|> Exercise
    work=ing
    handle=_geometric_construction_of_1_over_A_new_ver_

    If $0$, $1$, and a value $A > 1$ are marked
    on a line...

    |> Image
        style=margin-top:0.8em;margin-bottom:0.8em;
        src=tmp-images/e5_line_with_0_1_and_A.svg

    ...how can we find the placement of...

    $$
    1 / A
    $$
    
    ...on the same line by a purely geometric construction?
    What about if $0 < A < 1$, $-1 < A < 0$, etc?

    |> Solution

        _Solution 1._
        The basic idea of the solution is to find
        an angle $\theta$ such that $A = \sec(\theta)$,
        if $|A| > 1$,
        or symmetrically, such that $A = \cos(\theta)$, if $|A| < 1$:

        |> Image
            src=tmp-images/e5_line_with_0_1_and_A_and_secant.svg

        |> Image
            src=tmp-images/e5_line_with_0_1_and_A_and_cos.svg

        (Cf. Exercise >>_secant_cosecant_intersections_.)
        We now proceed with
        step-by-step
        compass-and-ruler 
        instructions for the two cases:

        __~ ~ case $|A| > 1$ ~ ~__

        _Step 1._ Find the midpoint between $0$ and $A$; for
        this, the procedure is to use two circles of equal radius:

        |> Image
            src=tmp-images/e5_line_with_0_1_and_A_Agt1_step1.svg

        _Step 2._ Draw a semicircle of radius $A/2$ whose
        center is that midpoint:

        |> Image
            src=tmp-images/e5_line_with_0_1_and_A_Agt1_step2.svg

        _Step 3._ Draw a circle of radius $1$ whose center
        is $(0, 0)$:

        |> Image
            src=tmp-images/e5_line_with_0_1_and_A_Agt1_step3.svg

        _Step 4._ Draw a line through the intersections
        of the two circles to find the position of $1/A$:

        |> Image
            src=tmp-images/e5_line_with_0_1_and_A_Agt1_step4.svg

        (The reason that the whole procedure works is that
        this triangle...

        |> Image
            src=tmp-images/e5_line_with_0_1_and_A_Agt1_step4_with_triangle.svg

        ...is actually right-angled at its upper corner
        by 
        the Peloponnesian circle theorem.
        This implies that the line from $(A, 0)$ to that
        corner is tangent to the circle of radius $1$, which is
        what we want/need.)

        $$
        \begin{gathered}
        x = 1 \\
        y = 1
        \end{gathered}
        $$

        __~ ~ case $|A| < 1$ ~ ~__

        _Step 1._ 
        With $|A| < 1$, start by
        marking points at equal distance to $A$ 
        by drawing a circle 
        centered at $A$,
        of any radius:

        |> Image
            src=tmp-images/e5_line_with_0_1_and_A_Alt1_step1_v3.svg

        _Step 2._ Use these points to draw a perpendicular 
        through $A$, similarly to the first step of
        the case $|A| > 1$:

        |> Image
            src=tmp-images/e5_line_with_0_1_and_A_Alt1_step2_v3.svg

        _Step 3._ 
        Draw a circle of radius $1$ centered at $(0, 0)$,
        and draw a line through $(0, 0)$ and the upper
        intersection of the circle with the perpendicular:

        |> Image
            src=tmp-images/e5_line_with_0_1_and_A_Alt1_step4_v3.svg

        _Step 4._ 
        Construct a perpendicular to this line
        at the point of intersection by repeating the
        process of the first two steps; 
        the intersection of that line with the
        main axis is $1/A$:

        |> Image
            src=tmp-images/e5_line_with_0_1_and_A_Alt1_step5_v3_smaller.svg

        |> StarDivider

        _Solution 2._ Note that for an angle

        $$
        \theta
        $$

        the values $$\tan\theta$$ and $$\cot\theta$$ can be
        found here and here, by Exercise >>_tan_cot_on_unit_circle_
        (this figure is just a recap of Exercise >>_tan_cot_on_unit_circle_):
        
        |> Image
            src=tmp-images/e5_line_with_0_1_and_A_tancot_without_A.svg

        However,

        $$
        \,\cot\theta = 1/\tan\theta,
        $$

        so if $\tan\theta = A$ then $\cot\theta = 1/A$, which
        gives rise to the following idea for a geometric construction
        of $1/A$, shown here for $0 < A < 1$ (though it works for all $A \in \rr$):

        |> Image
            src=tmp-images/e5_line_with_0_1_and_A_tancot_idea.svg

        Or, symmetrically, we can treat $A$ as $\cot\theta$, from
        which $\tan\theta$ becomes $1/A$:

        !! In this solution we use the fact
        !! that
        !! $$
        !! \tan\theta = 1/\cot\theta
        !! $$
        !! as well as geometric interpretations of
        !! tangent and cotangent
        !! elaborated in Exercise >>_tan_cot_on_unit_circle_,
        !! namely, that

        !! __$\tan\theta$ is the $y$-coordinate of the
        !! intersection of the line through $(0, 0)$
        !! and $(\cos\theta, \sin\theta)$ with the
        !! line $x = 1$__

        !! and

        !! __$\cot\theta$ is the $x$-coordinate of the
        !! intersection of the line through $(0, 0)$
        !! and $(\cos\theta, \sin\theta)$ with the
        !! line $y = 1$__

        !! which gives us (basic idea):

        !! |> Image
        !!     src=tmp-images/e5_line_with_0_1_and_A_tancot_idea.svg
        !!     style=margin-bottom:-1em;

        !! Or (symmetric basic idea):

        |> Image
            src=tmp-images/e5_line_with_0_1_and_A_tancot_idea_symmetric.svg

        !! (By the way this second construction 
        The second diagram
        can also
        be understood in terms of “materializing” the slope
        of a line of slope $1/A$ by scaling a “rise over run”
        triangle until the run hits $1$...

        |> Image
            src=tmp-images/e5_line_with_0_1_and_A_tancot_idea_symmetric_slope_explained.svg

        ...like so; 
        !! symmetrically, speaking of which, the first construction can
        and symmetrically, the first diagram can 
        be understood in terms of scaling a “rise over run”
        triangle until the _rise_ hits $1$...

        |> Image
            src=tmp-images/e5_line_with_0_1_and_A_tancot_idea_slope_explained.svg

        ...like so, since “$\te{run} = {\te{rise}\over \te{slope}}$”
        implies “$\te{run} = {1\over \te{slope}}$” by the
        time ‘rise’ hits $1$, implying “$\te{run} = {1\over A}$”, since the 
        slope is $A$.

        Complete compass-and-ruler constructions can
        easily be reconstituted from the above diagrams,
        and we hereby declare the second solution finished!!
